Subject: let a fake root keep its capabilities when the kernel locks KEEPCAPS

Redis 8 and Valkey exit on the phone with "setpriv: keep process
capabilities failed: Operation not permitted". Android's zygote starts apps
with SECBIT_KEEP_CAPS_LOCKED (securebits 0x2f, read on the device), so the
kernel answers prctl(PR_SET_KEEPCAPS) with EPERM, set or clear. setpriv
--reuid ... --clear-groups (Redis 7.4+, Redis 8, Valkey entrypoints) calls it
first; patch 0011 only forgave the later setgroups/capset/bounding/ambient
calls, which the host cannot show because a Linux host does not lock the bit.

fake_id0 now answers PR_SET_KEEPCAPS for a fake root (euid 0 or the kept
capability flag) as the kernel would without the lock: it records the keep
flag it already uses for setuid/setgid/setgroups and turns that one EPERM
into success. Nothing real is granted: the secure bits and the kernel's
credentials are unchanged, and a process that is not fake root still gets
the refusal.

